WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.660
ANNA KATE REAGAN:
Good afternoon.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.420
My name is Anna Kate Reagan.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.400
I'm majoring in
American Studies,

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.160
and I'm currently a sophomore.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.830
Our speaker today,
Robert A. Sirico,

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:30.980
is co-founder and president
of the Acton Institute

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.470
for the Study of
Religion and Liberty

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:37.610
and the pastor at Sacred Heart
of Jesus Parish and Academy.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.890
He received his Master
of Divinity degree

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.410
from the Catholic
University of America,

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.920
following undergraduate study
at the University of Southern

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.380
California and the
University of London.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.600
He has written for
numerous publications,

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.440
including The New York Times,
The Wall Street Journal,

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.620
Forbes, The Financial
Times, The Detroit News,

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.420
and The Washington Times.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.120
He has been a guest on
numerous television and radio

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:08.840
programs on CNN, ABC, CBS,
NPR, and the BBC, among others.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.010
A member of the Mount
Pelerin Society,

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.040
the American
Academy of Religion,

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.130
and the Philadelphia
Society, Reverend Sirico

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:18.980
is the author of Defending
the Free Market: The Moral

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.080
Case for a Free Economy.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.055
Please join me in
welcoming Reverend Robert

00:01:24.055 --> 00:01:26.179
Sirico to the stage.

00:01:26.179 --> 00:01:27.616
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.837
ROBERT A. SIRICO: Well, it's
good to be in Jerusalem.

00:01:38.837 --> 00:01:40.665
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.890
I am really so delighted
to be with you tonight.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:51.420
And I have a number of students
from my parish school, Sacred

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.190
Heart Academy, over here.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.440
There just seems to be a
nice symbience, especially

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.010
given the fact that--

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:59.700
oh, I'll just guess
80% of our teachers

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.190
are graduates of
Hillsdale as well.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:03.388
So it's a--

00:02:03.388 --> 00:02:04.304
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:04.304 --> 00:02:05.220
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:07.880
--good product here.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:15.360
When I was in seminary,
lo those many years ago,

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.850
taught by the Apostle
Paul, I think--

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.250
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.670
--part of our curriculum,
part of our formation

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.560
wasn't just academic.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.730
There was that dimension to it.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.520
But it's also pastoral.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.720
How do you deal with people?

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:38.550
And part of the work that I
was assigned to do in one year

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.590
was to work with the poor.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:43.590
And this meant I was at
Catholic University of America

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.480
in Washington, DC.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.070
And we would go
and serve the poor

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.910
in the inner city of the inner
city of Washington, DC, which

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.920
meant Anacostia.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.230
And here's how it went.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:03.030
On Fridays, a seminary
buddy and myself

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.250
would go, get on the train--

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.680
subway and go to Anacostia.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.060
And there, we met a sister,
who ran a soup kitchen

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.650
in the basement of
a Baptist church.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.230
And she was very
entrepreneurial.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:20.670
What she did was she
contacted all of the churches

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:26.670
in Chevy Chase and
the various areas

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.470
surrounding Washington, DC.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.960
And so she'd write to
the Presbyterians' lady

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.290
auxiliary, or the ladies
of the Baptist group,

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:37.780
or the Catholic Rosary
Society, or whatever it was.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:44.310
And she'd say, we need from
you 300 meals once a year.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:48.000
Your ladies can make
food for 300 people,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.200
and we will serve them.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:54.870
So I imagined what went on was
that the ladies in the Holy

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.290
Rosary Society of
Chevy Chase, Maryland,

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.660
would give the recipe
to their maids.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:01.540
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:07.140
And the maids would
prepare the dinner

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.410
and hand it to the chauffeurs,
who would drive it down to us

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:11.250
in Anacostia.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.083
Now, I don't know that
that really happened,

00:04:13.083 --> 00:04:17.100
but this was my Brooklyn
fantasy going on here.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.339
And the seminarians
would arrive.

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:24.630
Sister said to us, now,
here's what we do here.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:29.340
Your job is to set up the
tables, put out the food,

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.140
make sure it's warm, heated.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:35.100
Put out the food, clean
up after you're done,

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.270
clean the whole thing up.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:40.140
And remember, whosoever
will may come,

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:43.540
we don't ask anybody any
questions about their status,

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.150
their life, or anything.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.000
They just come, and they
get something to eat.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.550
And so that was the
agreement, and we did that.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.380
And usually what would
happen is we'd set it up,

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:54.360
and we'd get it going.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.860
And then we could
sit down and maybe

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:02.010
have a meal with people who
were there and different chores.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:07.570
I remember once, I was
bundling together some garbage

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:10.300
and went out to put
it in the trash bin.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:16.840
And I noticed that a cab pulled
up, and two ladies got out.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:19.420
And they had shopping
bags with them.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.010
And I just greeted them.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:21.810
I said, hi.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:23.670
She said, are we time to eat?

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:24.212
I said, yeah.

00:05:24.212 --> 00:05:26.128
She said, can we get to
the front of the line,

00:05:26.128 --> 00:05:27.190
because we have to go.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:29.830
There's a sale we need to make.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:30.730
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:33.910
I said, whosoever will may come.

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:34.870
Sure, ladies.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:36.260
Come on.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:39.250
So they went downstairs,
and we would work this.

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:45.130
And one Friday, Joe and
myself, my classmate,

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:46.510
were finishing up.

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:49.000
And we hadn't eaten
anything because the Baptist

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:55.520
ladies sent chili on a Friday
in lent, so good Catholic boys.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.210
And I said, Joe, when we
get back to the seminary,

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:00.880
the sisters are going to
have closed down the kitchen.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.880
So if we're going to
get something to eat,

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.650
we'd better get something
before we get home.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:08.680
And I said, there's a
fish and chips place

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.180
I saw right down the street.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:13.570
Maybe we can go there
and get something to eat.

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:15.215
And he said, OK,
we'll go do that.

00:06:15.215 --> 00:06:16.840
So we went to the
fish and chips place.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:18.700
It was after the lunch hour.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:22.540
And as we walked in,
there was a man kind

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:25.120
of cleaning up the tables
and sweeping out things

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.790
and a young girl helping him.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:31.300
And he came over to the
table and took our order.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:33.590
And I noticed he passed it
through the kitchen window.

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:38.080
And there was a woman in there,
and she was frying up the fish.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.370
And immediately I realized,
oh, this is a family.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.120
And then it dawned on
me, this is a family

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:47.540
who lives in this neighborhood.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:51.010
This is a family that lives
in this neighborhood who

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:55.830
run a business in
this neighborhood.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.820
And I am their
competitor on Fridays.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:00.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:04.500
The advantage I had
is that we didn't

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:05.580
have to pay for our food.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.200
We didn't pay for the insurance.

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:09.630
We didn't pay any of
the salary to anybody.

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:10.590
Sister worked for free.

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:11.450
We worked for free.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:15.750
No light bills,
nothing like that.

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:18.820
We had no problem.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:24.070
This family, on Fridays,
had less customers

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:26.693
because we were there
doing charitable work

00:07:26.693 --> 00:07:27.610
in their neighborhood.

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:32.020
And we never asked anybody who
came to us what they needed.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:34.870
And I'm sitting there eating
with my seminary buddy,

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:41.170
and I said, Joe, a thought
has just crossed my mind.

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:44.350
And he said, what?

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:46.460
I said, we are this
guy's competitors.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:49.990
We're making life more
difficult for this family

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:51.558
in this neighborhood.

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:55.690
He said, you're a heretic.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:56.658
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:00.670
I said, no, no, think about it.

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:02.420
He said, well, what
are we supposed to do?

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:03.705
He was from Brooklyn, too.

00:08:03.705 --> 00:08:05.694
(BROOKLYN ACCENT) What
are we supposed to do?

00:08:05.694 --> 00:08:06.682
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:10.700
I said, I'm not sure.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:12.430
This thought is just
occurring to me.

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:14.500
It's a very
uncomfortable thought,

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:17.830
because we have all the best
intentions in the world.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:21.050
We want to help these people.

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:24.040
But the problem is, we
don't know these people.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:27.220
We don't know the kind
of help that they need,

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:28.630
and we don't ask them.

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:30.520
We don't ask them to
contribute anything.

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:32.228
We don't ask them to
bring a little food.

00:08:32.228 --> 00:08:34.330
We don't ask them
to help us set up.

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:37.183
We don't ask them
to help us clean up.

00:08:37.183 --> 00:08:38.600
We don't know
anything about them.

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:42.500
There's no rationing
of any sort going on

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:49.670
that a price system would
normally provide for.

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:53.220
That has always remained
a question for me.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:54.690
How do we combat that?

00:08:54.690 --> 00:09:00.280
So my topic today is to
talk about Christianity,

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:03.110
social justice, and poverty.

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:04.880
And that thought
immediately came

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:09.470
to my mind, the complexities
of serving people, serving

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:10.880
the vulnerable,
serving the poor.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:15.870
You see, it's not
just good intentions.

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:19.440
We need something else.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.090
If we're talking
about feeding people,

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:24.510
if we're talking about
people who are poor,

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:26.310
then we're talking
about economics.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:30.360
We have to bring sound
economics to questions

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:32.040
of good intentions.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:35.220
And if we don't, we can make a
real mess of the whole thing.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.890
Somebody wrote a whole
book on this called Poverty

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:41.220
That Hurts or Kills.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:44.490
So that was my seminary
experience in the soup kitchen

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:46.530
long before I founded
the Acton Institute.

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:48.840
But the Acton Institute,
in a lot of ways,

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.030
grew out of that
concern that I began

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:54.720
to see on many, many,
many levels, not just soup

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:57.570
kitchens in inner cities.

00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:02.582
We need something more
than good intentions.

00:10:02.582 --> 00:10:04.790
I want to talk first about
the nature of Christianity

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:07.040
because that's
the moral backdrop

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:09.710
in which a lot of people debate
these kinds of questions.

00:10:09.710 --> 00:10:13.520
Even if it's not an
explicit religious argument,

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:16.130
you notice that when we
have political arguments

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:20.960
about the costs of education,
the cost of health care,

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:25.340
the questions of
shelter, and clothing,

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:28.730
and the well-being
of people, it all

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:34.160
takes place in front of the
backdrop of a moral question.

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:36.950
What ought we be
doing as a society?

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:40.440
What kind of a society
is a moral society?

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:43.580
What kind of a
society is a society

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.190
appropriate to human dignity?

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:48.110
That's really the
question that we're

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:50.670
facing in many, many ways.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:52.760
And Christianity, in
particular-- of course,

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:56.390
when I say Christianity, I
mean it in its Judeo-Christian

00:10:56.390 --> 00:11:02.390
reality because Christianity
is the younger daughter

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:04.550
of Judaism--

00:11:04.550 --> 00:11:07.430
the Jewish people,
the Jewish covenant

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:11.610
being the inheritance
of Christianity.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:15.570
And the whole anthropology, the
whole view of the human person

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:19.170
that comes out of
this Biblical idea--

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:20.940
and it's really
incredible when you

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:26.160
think about what
these ideas formed

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:29.565
and what they grew into over
a period of roughly 2,000

00:11:29.565 --> 00:11:32.190
to 3,000 years.

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:36.090
In Christianity, what is the
main claim of Christianity

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:38.650
is that God became one of us.

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:45.510
He joined at a certain point in
time and entered human history,

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:49.020
that the eternal God
commingled with the human race

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:52.050
and not from outside the
world, but he broke out

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:55.230
of the material world
in the incarnation

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:57.930
when he was born in Bethlehem.

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:00.810
Think about what that means.

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.310
That "In the beginning
was the Word,

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:07.590
and the Word was with God,"
the prologue of John's Gospel,

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:12.300
and "the Word became flesh."

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:15.570
This enfleshment is
the core foundation.

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:19.530
It's the way in which
Christianity views

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:21.210
everything else about itself--

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:24.810
its sacraments, its
social engagement.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:27.390
It has to do with the goodness
of the material world.

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:32.190
And in a real sense,
this is a reiteration

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:33.210
of the book of Genesis.

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:34.620
This is why, I think,
it's very important

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.390
to keep Judaism and
Christianity together.

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:37.590
You can't separate the two.

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:44.550
In the Garden of Eden, when
God created man and woman,

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:49.380
He created them after having
formed all things from nothing

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:52.830
into being and pronounced
the material world good,

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:56.880
and then creates man, who is a
composite of Heaven and Earth,

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:59.790
the dust of the ground
and the breath of life,

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:03.420
and then gives man and
woman, as His first words

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:08.730
spoken to the human race in the
Garden of Eden, the vocation.

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:13.700
And that vocation is
to tend the garden.

00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:14.950
Notice, by the way--

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:16.610
we won't get into
environmentalism--

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:21.380
but notice, by the way, that it
was a garden and not a jungle.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:23.570
And what's the difference?

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:25.760
The garden needs tending.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:31.970
The human being is not
unnatural to the environment.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:36.320
Some people will argue that
we're the only unnatural thing

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:37.200
on the planet.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:38.870
Everything else needs
to be preserved,

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:41.810
but human life is dispensable.

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:43.260
But I digress.

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:46.560
And, yet, it informs
what we're talking about.

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:50.037
Because this view, both
of the Old Testament

00:13:50.037 --> 00:13:51.620
in the Book of
Genesis, and in the New

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:54.650
Testament in the Doctrine
of the Incarnation,

00:13:54.650 --> 00:13:58.250
says something about the
sanctity of the material world,

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:01.880
that God had an intention
for the material world

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:02.720
that He created.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:07.010
He reiterates that intention
in the incarnation of His Son.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:11.960
And what begins to happen,
what begins to unfold

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:14.960
is not just an idea,
an abstraction,

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:16.950
but an institution.

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:19.420
It's called the Church.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:23.320
The Church confronts
society and culture,

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:28.760
and builds culture,
and confronts history

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:31.085
with the message of her Savior.

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:39.160
Part of this message
is the dignity

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:41.740
of the human person, an
anthropological vision

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:45.340
that each human being, from the
first moment of their existence

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:51.160
until their natural demise,
is the possessor of a dignity

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:53.530
that each person
is unrepeatable.

00:14:53.530 --> 00:14:55.810
Each person is unique.

00:14:55.810 --> 00:15:00.340
Each person bears the image
of God, the Imago Dei.

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:06.620
Each person is created in that
image of God for eternity.

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:08.520
That is who the human person is.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:12.340
Now that idea began
to be fleshed out

00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:14.830
in an institutional form.

00:15:14.830 --> 00:15:17.170
And what that meant was
that when Christianity

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:21.190
began to confront human
beings in their vulnerability,

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:23.800
whether they were widows,
whether they were orphans,

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:27.770
whether they were profoundly
handicapped or near death,

00:15:27.770 --> 00:15:32.000
whether they were diseased,
regardless of their race,

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:34.610
that the Christian would
always confront that person as

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:36.800
though they were
confronting Christ himself,

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:39.773
because Jesus said that.

00:15:39.773 --> 00:15:41.190
"To the extent
that you do it unto

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:43.800
one of the least of
these, my brethren,

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:46.080
you have done it unto me."

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:49.110
So the encounter with
the poor and the mind

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:52.420
and the assumption of the
Church is an encounter

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:54.340
with Christ Himself.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:58.870
And we see this not just
as a theological concept,

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:01.240
but we see it very early
in the Church's history.

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:04.580
Even right outside of the
text of the Scriptures

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:07.920
we begin to see it.

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:14.080
There was a plague that broke
out in the city of Antioch.

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:18.920
And there is some
historical material

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:23.660
related to this, where the
legion guard, the captain,

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:26.670
whatever he was wrote back
to Rome and said, look,

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:29.310
there is a plague
that's broken out.

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:30.830
It's spreading.

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:35.270
I've pulled back the troops
so that they wouldn't

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:37.010
get caught in the contagion.

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:39.732
Of course, the politicians
had already left.

00:16:39.732 --> 00:16:40.616
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:47.900
He said, and even people's
families, even physicians,

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:51.380
are leaving people
because of this contagion.

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:55.100
But one strange
thing is happening.

00:16:55.100 --> 00:17:02.460
There are a group of people,
this cult following Christ,

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:05.349
who are staying with the dying.

00:17:05.349 --> 00:17:10.000
And they're tending to them
at risk to their own lives.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:15.660
And this is just a soldier
reporting to his superiors

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:16.180
right there.

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:18.869
Of course, you remember,
in the Acts of the Apostles

00:17:18.869 --> 00:17:21.150
in Antioch, that's
where they were called

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:23.730
Christians for the first time.

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:27.930
They're living out what
Christ did Himself.

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:31.920
This intuition to associate
oneself with the dying,

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:36.580
with the vulnerable, with
the poor, with the broken,

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:39.700
because of Christianity's
belief in the Incarnation,

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:42.940
the seriousness with
which God took history,

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:45.700
and culture, and
the material world,

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:48.990
began to form itself
into institutions, first

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:51.650
very small, very informal.

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:54.760
It would happen, oh, I think,
in the early Middle Ages

00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:58.550
that people, when plagues would
break out or people were dying,

00:17:58.550 --> 00:18:01.840
they would bring
them to convents.

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:04.328
And very often, the convents
would have some extra rooms.

00:18:04.328 --> 00:18:05.620
And they would bring the dying.

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:08.860
And they say, we don't know
what to do with these people.

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:10.853
Medicine was very primitive.

00:18:10.853 --> 00:18:12.020
Would you take care of them?

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:15.970
And so they would
receive them in,

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:18.140
and the sisters would
take care of them.

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:21.070
And in the process of
taking care of them,

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:25.120
they would develop
systems of sanitation,

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:31.210
cleaning, refreshing the
bed, keeping air circulating.

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:33.370
The sisters and
monks did this too.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:36.970
They began also to prepare these
very primitive recipes that

00:18:36.970 --> 00:18:38.620
were medicinals.

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:39.740
Today, we enjoy them.

00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:41.770
They're called
limoncello, sambuca--

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:42.694
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:44.780 --> 00:18:49.530
--chartreuse, all these
wonderful digestifs.

00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:51.250
But these really,
all of these things

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:54.820
were originally medicines,
herbal medicines

00:18:54.820 --> 00:18:55.660
to help people.

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:59.612
And hit and miss, they
began to work with people.

00:18:59.612 --> 00:19:01.820
And they found some things
worked better than others.

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:07.520
And this was first
called hospices and then

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:11.390
later in more institutionalized
forms, hospitals.

00:19:11.390 --> 00:19:15.900
The invention of hospitals
is a Christian invention.

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:21.090
Not only that,
but consider this,

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:25.530
that institutionalized
and internationalized

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:29.490
organized charity is the
result of Christianity because

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:31.500
of the organizational
system of the Church

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:36.130
and the various places
that the Church existed.

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:41.530
And the idea that charity
was done not to people

00:19:41.530 --> 00:19:44.570
because they were
Christians, charity

00:19:44.570 --> 00:19:47.670
was done because
we were Christians,

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:50.790
because we could
encounter Christ anywhere.

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:53.100
Human life is sacred.

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:58.200
These ideas came out of this
anthropology, of this cosmology

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:00.120
that we find in the Old
Testament and the New

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:01.147
Testament.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:07.680
Universities come out
of this experience

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:14.410
if you read any of the
great historians of ideas.

00:20:14.410 --> 00:20:18.950
Today, we are harangued
by a secular culture that

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:21.920
is trying to tell the
Church how to do charity

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:25.460
when institutionalized
charity is our invention.

00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:27.500
And all they have
are secularized,

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:32.720
desiccated forms of charity
that aren't even that generous

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:36.560
and certainly aren't
even that effective.

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:38.860
And here's why.

00:20:38.860 --> 00:20:44.320
Because what Christian charity
did in connection with the poor

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:47.965
was to see the person
as a whole being.

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:53.710
That is to say that
they were material.

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:56.630
They had needs.

00:20:56.630 --> 00:21:00.700
If they were hungry, or if they
were sick, or if they tired,

00:21:00.700 --> 00:21:03.460
or if they were whatever
it happened to be,

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:06.880
to tend to those material needs,
but that there was something

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:08.720
more than that.

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:10.860
They were also
transcendent beings.

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:14.540
They were also beings destined
for eternal relationship

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:16.070
with God.

00:21:16.070 --> 00:21:18.290
They were spiritual beings.

00:21:18.290 --> 00:21:22.990
And this holistic view of
who the human person was

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:28.100
gives a fuller understanding of
how to deal with that person.

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:30.370
In other words, to put
it a different way,

00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:35.690
unlike secularist
forms of charity,

00:21:35.690 --> 00:21:39.688
we don't treat people just
as though they were animals,

00:21:39.688 --> 00:21:41.105
just as though
they were material.

00:21:43.890 --> 00:21:47.040
When an animal is cold, you
take it in out of the cold

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:48.030
and put it in the barn.

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:50.460
When it's hungry,
you give it some hay.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:53.350
When it's thirsty, you
bring it to the trough

00:21:53.350 --> 00:21:56.350
because that's the total
existence of an animal.

00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:58.530
It's material.

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:03.060
The human being
transcends the material.

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:07.740
They are material, but there's
something more than physical.

00:22:07.740 --> 00:22:11.250
And because of
that, there is love.

00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:12.450
There is bonding.

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:14.280
There's an
understanding that when

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:17.190
a person comes to us
in need, that there's

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.380
a whole context that
must be provided

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:21.180
and not just the material.

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:24.240
The material,
again, is important,

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:26.540
but it's not the whole thing.

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:29.210
And the secularist
versions that only,

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:32.300
when they confront
poverty, very often

00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:34.850
in the form of a young
woman who has become

00:22:34.850 --> 00:22:38.450
pregnant outside of marriage,
and has been kicked out

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:41.060
of her home, and doesn't
have a high school education,

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:44.500
what will the welfare state do?

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:48.290
It will give her an abortion, or
it will give her contraception.

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:49.540
It will give her food stamps.

00:22:49.540 --> 00:22:51.790
It will give her
some counseling,

00:22:51.790 --> 00:22:53.780
but it won't bond with her.

00:22:53.780 --> 00:22:56.440
It can't bond with her.

00:22:56.440 --> 00:23:01.150
And this is why Christianity
knows something,

00:23:01.150 --> 00:23:05.790
not just about poverty,
but about the poor.

00:23:05.790 --> 00:23:07.920
But there's something
else embedded

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:12.690
in the Biblical view of human
life that's very important.

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:15.330
And that is that the material
world is sacred and can

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:18.720
be the occasion
for honoring God,

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:21.630
and that the bearer
of the image of God

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:24.000
also is bound to
the world primarily

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:27.450
by their use of
reason, and that we

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:31.890
can work in this material
world so as to produce

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:34.440
more than we consume.

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:36.870
And this understanding
of humanity

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:43.470
is very different than the
zero-sum form of humanophobia

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:46.290
that we see at
the United Nations

00:23:46.290 --> 00:23:50.190
and very often in the
halls of Congress, the fear

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:53.070
that if there are more
human beings on this planet

00:23:53.070 --> 00:23:58.980
there's going to be less of
the pie for us to all eat.

00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:01.020
It only sees the human
person as a mouth

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:04.950
that consumes and not also
as a mind that produces,

00:24:04.950 --> 00:24:07.020
that is creative.

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:13.070
How can it be that a
man who has 10 pigs

00:24:13.070 --> 00:24:16.520
is richer than a
man that has one,

00:24:16.520 --> 00:24:20.360
but a man who has 10
children is seen as poorer

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:22.430
than a man that has one?

00:24:22.430 --> 00:24:28.010
Now we see the human person
as the source of creativity,

00:24:28.010 --> 00:24:32.480
that man, as St. John
Paul II once said,

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:37.460
"Man is man's
greatest resource."

00:24:37.460 --> 00:24:41.120
And that to marginalize
the dignity of human life

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:43.490
and to see human life
or more human life,

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:48.840
children as a threat to
economic productivity

00:24:48.840 --> 00:24:54.150
is a fallacy and a
dangerous one at that.

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:56.400
How do we counter this?

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:58.650
What do we do in the face of it?

00:25:02.750 --> 00:25:05.670
We deal with people who we know.

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:08.940
We bond with people who
have needs and needs

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:12.750
that, because we know
them, we know their needs.

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:17.330
This, in my tradition, is called
the principle of subsidiarity.

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:22.130
In the Reformed tradition,
the great Reform theologian

00:25:22.130 --> 00:25:25.830
and statesman Abraham Kuyper
called it sphere sovereignty.

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:27.800
Now there are
technical differences

00:25:27.800 --> 00:25:31.040
between sphere sovereignty
and subsidiarity,

00:25:31.040 --> 00:25:33.710
but let me articulate
to you what I know best,

00:25:33.710 --> 00:25:36.350
that this is in the
form of subsidiarity,

00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:37.770
what it's talking about.

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:39.680
It'll sound very
familiar to you.

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:41.960
Basically what
subsidiarity says is

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:44.480
that needs are best
met at the most

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:46.190
local level of their existence.

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:52.850
Political societies
of higher complexity

00:25:52.850 --> 00:25:57.350
ought not to intervene
into lower forms of society

00:25:57.350 --> 00:26:00.680
except in cases
of dire emergency

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:03.670
and even then only temporarily.

00:26:03.670 --> 00:26:06.580
To put it simply,
needs are best met

00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:09.730
at the most local level
of their existence.

00:26:09.730 --> 00:26:13.210
To put it simply,
if I have a need,

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:16.960
I have the first
responsibility to meet my need.

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:21.560
But if there's some collapse in
me for some reason or another,

00:26:21.560 --> 00:26:23.410
then it's my family's
responsibility

00:26:23.410 --> 00:26:24.650
to respond to that need.

00:26:24.650 --> 00:26:28.510
And if there's a collapse
there, then our extended family,

00:26:28.510 --> 00:26:31.960
our cousins, our relatives,
maybe our neighbors, maybe

00:26:31.960 --> 00:26:35.530
our churches, maybe other
mediating institutions

00:26:35.530 --> 00:26:38.920
in society that are independent
of the state, all of these

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:40.660
need to be the first
to be permitted

00:26:40.660 --> 00:26:43.930
to act on behalf of
those who have needs,

00:26:43.930 --> 00:26:47.560
precisely because these
are the people who

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:51.330
know the people best.

00:26:51.330 --> 00:26:54.030
They know if there's a
real need or if you're just

00:26:54.030 --> 00:26:55.980
putting on an act.

00:26:55.980 --> 00:27:05.180
They know what that need is, if
it's a meal, or if it's love,

00:27:05.180 --> 00:27:10.790
or if it's counsel, or if it's
a good swift kick in the behind,

00:27:10.790 --> 00:27:13.100
because we all have
different needs

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:16.100
and unless we know each other.

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:19.220
The real sinister thing--

00:27:19.220 --> 00:27:23.060
in Italian, "sinistra"
means left, by the way.

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:24.940
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:28.350
The real "sinistra,"
the real sinister thing

00:27:28.350 --> 00:27:32.290
here is that the welfare
state in the name of the poor

00:27:32.290 --> 00:27:34.450
disables the poor very often.

00:27:34.450 --> 00:27:36.970
It disincentivizes
the rest of society

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:39.790
to act first because
there's an intuition.

00:27:39.790 --> 00:27:42.820
Because resources
are scarce, there's

00:27:42.820 --> 00:27:46.400
an intuition that says,
well, if their need is going

00:27:46.400 --> 00:27:49.420
to be met by this group
of people, or this agency,

00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:53.630
or whatever, then I'll
go and do something else.

00:27:53.630 --> 00:27:55.610
And look at what happens.

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:59.120
Society begins to fragment.

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:01.700
People are no longer
bound together

00:28:01.700 --> 00:28:04.460
by bonds of concern
and love in the way

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:07.640
that Alexis de Tocqueville
saw when he visited the United

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:13.670
States and documented it in
Democracy in America, Americans

00:28:13.670 --> 00:28:18.140
always organizing themselves
in some kind of social group

00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:21.330
to meet various kinds of needs.

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:25.150
Now authentic
community is the result

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:29.410
of a vibrant and strict
implementation of subsidiarity.

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:34.510
This is the solution,
the wealth creation

00:28:34.510 --> 00:28:39.010
of a free economy combined
with vibrant communities

00:28:39.010 --> 00:28:43.510
of moral concern without regard
to specific denomination.

00:28:43.510 --> 00:28:45.950
I grew up in Brooklyn, New York.

00:28:45.950 --> 00:28:49.330
And we had as many different
religions going on,

00:28:49.330 --> 00:28:52.300
mostly Jewish and Catholic--

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:54.337
OK, and a few Protestants
were over there,

00:28:54.337 --> 00:28:55.920
but they lived in
other neighborhoods.

00:28:55.920 --> 00:28:57.864
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:03.260
But we worked
together on projects

00:29:03.260 --> 00:29:06.550
that we saw as
important because we

00:29:06.550 --> 00:29:09.500
were neighbors to one another.

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:14.530
We had a multi-ethnic experience
without a government mandate

00:29:14.530 --> 00:29:17.230
to have one because
we were generally

00:29:17.230 --> 00:29:21.440
left free to form communities.

00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:24.600
Frederic Bastiat in his
wonderful little book--

00:29:24.600 --> 00:29:26.010
and any students here--

00:29:26.010 --> 00:29:27.010
well, this is Hillsdale.

00:29:27.010 --> 00:29:28.930
You probably already read it--

00:29:28.930 --> 00:29:30.790
The Law by Frederic Bastiat.

00:29:30.790 --> 00:29:31.990
It's online.

00:29:31.990 --> 00:29:34.977
It'll take you an hour
and a half to read.

00:29:34.977 --> 00:29:37.060
It's a wonderful book
because it doesn't teach you

00:29:37.060 --> 00:29:38.420
what to think.

00:29:38.420 --> 00:29:40.360
It teaches you how to think.

00:29:40.360 --> 00:29:43.710
And one of the things he
talks about, fraternité--

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:47.500
fraternité, fraternity,
brotherhood.

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:49.390
One of the things
he says in that book

00:29:49.390 --> 00:29:54.670
is, you can't force
people to be brothers.

00:29:54.670 --> 00:29:58.480
To the extent you force them,
they're no longer brothers--

00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:00.940
that it has to be
something that is free,

00:30:00.940 --> 00:30:03.400
something that is
invited, something that

00:30:03.400 --> 00:30:08.070
is compelled not by
government, but by morality.

00:30:12.250 --> 00:30:16.120
People talk a lot about
social justice today.

00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:22.030
It's a well-used
and abused phrase.

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:27.430
Let's remember that
Aristotle gives us

00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:31.570
a very clear definition
of what justice is.

00:30:31.570 --> 00:30:36.250
Justice is to be treated
in accord with desert.

00:30:36.250 --> 00:30:40.410
To the extent that
there is social justice,

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:43.280
it is society
treating society, that

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:45.500
is, people,
communities of people

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:48.560
treating each other as
they deserve to be treated.

00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:51.140
But let's be very clear.

00:30:51.140 --> 00:30:56.400
Societies don't act
unless people act.

00:30:56.400 --> 00:30:59.700
Societies can't be
informed or imbued

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:02.950
with either vice or virtue.

00:31:02.950 --> 00:31:08.470
It's people who are the
vicious or virtuous.

00:31:08.470 --> 00:31:12.500
So the extent that we're
talking about social justice,

00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:15.460
we need to be very careful
that people aren't smuggling

00:31:15.460 --> 00:31:19.840
into that idea the notion
of socialist justice, which

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:24.610
couldn't be more of an
oxymoron than anything else.

00:31:24.610 --> 00:31:26.590
We see that historically.

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:28.480
We see it today in Venezuela.

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:30.350
We see it in North Korea.

00:31:30.350 --> 00:31:32.800
We see it everywhere
that socialism

00:31:32.800 --> 00:31:39.100
has been attempted in our world,
the injustice of brutal regimes

00:31:39.100 --> 00:31:44.230
to decide for people how
their lives should go.

00:31:44.230 --> 00:31:49.900
The real temptation in a
morally sensitive heart

00:31:49.900 --> 00:31:52.240
is to respond to people
in the simplest way

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:54.930
that they know how.

00:31:54.930 --> 00:31:57.460
Let the government do it.

00:31:57.460 --> 00:32:04.390
The problem is, the simplest
way isn't always the moral way.

00:32:04.390 --> 00:32:09.040
The real way to build
a society that is just,

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:12.010
a society that is imbued
with a sense of something

00:32:12.010 --> 00:32:18.670
more than justice, a sense
of charity, a sense of love,

00:32:18.670 --> 00:32:21.610
is to allow people to get to
know one another, to act freely

00:32:21.610 --> 00:32:25.480
and to be formed by
our moral vision.

00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:30.130
This is not an easy undertaking.

00:32:30.130 --> 00:32:34.330
The depressing thing
is that the very people

00:32:34.330 --> 00:32:36.310
in our society,
religious leaders

00:32:36.310 --> 00:32:40.450
in particular and
business leaders as well,

00:32:40.450 --> 00:32:44.020
who ought to understand
how this works very often

00:32:44.020 --> 00:32:47.310
don't understand how this works.

00:32:47.310 --> 00:32:50.010
To see religious leaders
abandoning their vocation

00:32:50.010 --> 00:32:51.390
to tend to the
needs of the poor,

00:32:51.390 --> 00:32:54.690
to tend to the needs
of Jesus Christ, who

00:32:54.690 --> 00:32:58.650
comes to us in
distressing disguises,

00:32:58.650 --> 00:33:03.390
and hand that over to the
government is abominable.

00:33:03.390 --> 00:33:07.590
And for business leaders to
likewise betray their vocation

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:11.070
to be creative and
productive with the talents

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:19.280
that they have been entrusted
with and seeking the benefit

00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:24.110
and advantages of the
state is an equal betrayal

00:33:24.110 --> 00:33:29.300
to the clergy, who abandoned
their vocation to the state.

00:33:29.300 --> 00:33:32.540
My friends, we need a
different kind of society.

00:33:32.540 --> 00:33:38.820
We need a society that
understands that piety is never

00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:41.920
a substitute for technique.

00:33:41.920 --> 00:33:43.930
I wish I had written
that line, but I didn't.

00:33:43.930 --> 00:33:44.980
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:44.980 --> 00:33:49.090
It's from Etienne Gilson,
who was a philosopher.

00:33:49.090 --> 00:33:51.930
He said, "If you want to build
the facade of a cathedral that

00:33:51.930 --> 00:33:57.330
represents the rising of the
soul to God like Notre Dame,

00:33:57.330 --> 00:34:00.660
you must first
understand geometry."

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:04.230
And then he said,
"Piety is never

00:34:04.230 --> 00:34:08.670
a substitute for technique,
for without technique piety

00:34:08.670 --> 00:34:14.219
is helpless to make use of
nature for the glory of God."

00:34:14.219 --> 00:34:17.960
We need a society that is
imbued with a moral sense

00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:21.900
and that has a high respect
for the technique of business,

00:34:21.900 --> 00:34:25.830
a moral sense of business,
a sense of business that

00:34:25.830 --> 00:34:28.650
is imbued with the
kind of anthropology

00:34:28.650 --> 00:34:30.870
that I spoke of earlier.

00:34:30.870 --> 00:34:33.060
C.S. Lewis spoke so
beautifully of this kind

00:34:33.060 --> 00:34:38.820
of anthropological vision, this
vision of the human person,

00:34:38.820 --> 00:34:41.489
when he said, "You have
never met a mere mortal."

00:34:41.489 --> 00:34:44.469
In The Weight of Glory,
he wrote these lines.

00:34:44.469 --> 00:34:46.870
"You have never
met a mere mortal.

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:50.830
Everyone with whom you have
ever come in contact, every shop

00:34:50.830 --> 00:34:53.560
girl from whom you've ever
purchased anything, anyone who

00:34:53.560 --> 00:34:57.100
you've ever had a fight with,
anyone you've ever quarreled

00:34:57.100 --> 00:35:00.820
with, anyone you have
ever loved or hated

00:35:00.820 --> 00:35:06.270
is either an immortal horror
or an everlasting splendor.

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:10.020
But you have never
met a mere mortal."

00:35:10.020 --> 00:35:13.340
And then Lewis went
on to say these words.

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:15.680
"The most sacred thing
that presents itself

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:18.620
to our senses next
to the blessed

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:23.120
sacrament itself
is our neighbor."

00:35:23.120 --> 00:35:28.840
This anthropology comes from
a Judeo-Christian vision

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:33.760
of how the world was formed,
how human life came to be,

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:38.950
and the dignity that each human
person possesses intrinsically

00:35:38.950 --> 00:35:41.240
in their very nature.

00:35:41.240 --> 00:35:43.660
This is the kind
of society that we

00:35:43.660 --> 00:35:48.640
must propose against the
ideology of division and class

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:53.270
warfare, against the
claims of Marxism,

00:35:53.270 --> 00:35:54.845
and socialism, and its variants.

00:35:57.750 --> 00:36:01.010
Now we must respond
to such ideas

00:36:01.010 --> 00:36:03.860
with the insights of someone
like Mother Teresa of Calcutta,

00:36:03.860 --> 00:36:09.240
who once said, "We should
not judge the rich.

00:36:09.240 --> 00:36:12.520
We do not advocate
class struggle.

00:36:12.520 --> 00:36:16.070
We advocate class
encounter, where

00:36:16.070 --> 00:36:21.460
the rich save the poor and
the poor save the rich."

00:36:21.460 --> 00:36:22.785
Thank you very much.

00:36:22.785 --> 00:36:24.485
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:41.230
SPEAKER 1: Thank
you, Reverend Sirico.

00:36:41.230 --> 00:36:43.880
We now have time for Q&amp;A.
If you have a question,

00:36:43.880 --> 00:36:45.894
please make your way
to the microphone.

00:36:52.186 --> 00:36:55.590
SPEAKER 2: Thank you.

00:36:55.590 --> 00:36:59.510
My question is, would it be
a good idea for all people,

00:36:59.510 --> 00:37:03.080
instead of just physicians only,
to ask themselves before taking

00:37:03.080 --> 00:37:05.958
action, first, do no harm?

00:37:05.958 --> 00:37:07.500
ROBERT A. SIRICO:
Not only would that

00:37:07.500 --> 00:37:09.042
be a good idea for
everyone to do it,

00:37:09.042 --> 00:37:11.640
it would be especially a
good idea for physicians

00:37:11.640 --> 00:37:13.770
to do it again today,
because I understand

00:37:13.770 --> 00:37:16.840
that's been taken out
of the Hippocratic oath.

00:37:16.840 --> 00:37:18.150
Did you take that out of--

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:20.582
did you take that vow?

00:37:20.582 --> 00:37:21.790
SPEAKER 2: First, do no harm?

00:37:21.790 --> 00:37:23.823
ROBERT A. SIRICO: Do no harm.

00:37:23.823 --> 00:37:24.990
SPEAKER 2: I tried to do it.

00:37:24.990 --> 00:37:25.850
And when you think of--

00:37:25.850 --> 00:37:27.100
ROBERT A. SIRICO: No,
but I mean as part

00:37:27.100 --> 00:37:28.410
of the Hippocratic oath.

00:37:28.410 --> 00:37:29.590
SPEAKER 2: No, sir.

00:37:29.590 --> 00:37:30.430
ROBERT A. SIRICO:
Are you a physician?

00:37:30.430 --> 00:37:30.790
SPEAKER 2: No.

00:37:30.790 --> 00:37:31.510
ROBERT A. SIRICO: Oh, I'm sorry.

00:37:31.510 --> 00:37:31.780
I thought you were.

00:37:31.780 --> 00:37:32.690
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:32.690 --> 00:37:33.190
No.

00:37:33.190 --> 00:37:36.220
I think they have removed
that in the monostich.

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:41.278
But, of course, yes, first do
no harm, absolutely, absolutely.

00:37:50.250 --> 00:37:52.630
SPEAKER 3: Should I go?

00:37:52.630 --> 00:37:57.750
Sir, in consonance with what you
have said so very, very well,

00:37:57.750 --> 00:37:59.510
I just want to make
a comment on a book

00:37:59.510 --> 00:38:04.610
by Samuel Cahill, an Irishman,
who wrote the book How

00:38:04.610 --> 00:38:07.580
the Irish Saved Civilization.

00:38:07.580 --> 00:38:10.640
And the last sentence
in that book,

00:38:10.640 --> 00:38:13.670
after describing all that
St. Patrick, and Colombo,

00:38:13.670 --> 00:38:19.520
and others did to rescue
Christianity on the continent,

00:38:19.520 --> 00:38:25.620
the last sentence is,
"The ills of our world

00:38:25.620 --> 00:38:30.020
will not be remedied by the
Romans, that is, the soldier,

00:38:30.020 --> 00:38:32.275
but by the saints."

00:38:32.275 --> 00:38:33.650
ROBERT A. SIRICO:
I believe that.

00:38:33.650 --> 00:38:34.410
I believe that.

00:38:34.410 --> 00:38:36.230
And I think we need--

00:38:36.230 --> 00:38:40.830
we just celebrated All Saints
Day on the 1st of November.

00:38:40.830 --> 00:38:41.960
We need to all be saints.

00:38:45.320 --> 00:38:46.910
SPEAKER 4: Hi.

00:38:46.910 --> 00:38:50.360
My question is concerning
wealthy Christians

00:38:50.360 --> 00:38:52.610
or specifically
Christian business

00:38:52.610 --> 00:38:57.685
owners who have the power to
set wages and especially--

00:38:57.685 --> 00:39:00.310
ROBERT A. SIRICO: Could you stop
right there just for a moment?

00:39:00.310 --> 00:39:01.320
I'm going to let you finish.

00:39:01.320 --> 00:39:01.950
SPEAKER 4: Yeah, absolutely.

00:39:01.950 --> 00:39:03.492
ROBERT A. SIRICO:
But I think there's

00:39:03.492 --> 00:39:08.260
an assumption that you just made
that is economically erroneous.

00:39:08.260 --> 00:39:15.450
So you said, business owners
who have the power to set wages.

00:39:15.450 --> 00:39:18.760
How are wages set?

00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:21.170
Does the fat businessman
with the cigar,

00:39:21.170 --> 00:39:25.288
and the tuxedo, and the top
hat say, we will pay them this?

00:39:25.288 --> 00:39:26.330
SPEAKER 4: Maybe if you--

00:39:26.330 --> 00:39:28.538
I'm sort of going to talk
about that in the question.

00:39:28.538 --> 00:39:30.230
You want to let
me finish it or--

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:31.772
ROBERT A. SIRICO:
Oh, sure, go ahead.

00:39:31.772 --> 00:39:37.610
SPEAKER 4: So I was
wondering, specifically

00:39:37.610 --> 00:39:42.980
regarding the employees, some
of whom might be in poverty

00:39:42.980 --> 00:39:46.940
or struggling with
costs of living,

00:39:46.940 --> 00:39:51.440
how does a business
owner sort of navigate

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:55.370
the teachings of capitalism,
which would advise them

00:39:55.370 --> 00:40:01.700
to maximize profit at all costs,
versus the teachings of Jesus

00:40:01.700 --> 00:40:06.200
Christ, which calls us to
alleviate suffering and help

00:40:06.200 --> 00:40:07.250
those in poverty?

00:40:07.250 --> 00:40:07.670
ROBERT A. SIRICO: Good.

00:40:07.670 --> 00:40:08.540
That's a good question.

00:40:08.540 --> 00:40:11.082
Now stay there, because I want
to walk through this with you.

00:40:11.082 --> 00:40:15.030
This is kind of like
my soup kitchen.

00:40:15.030 --> 00:40:17.810
It's filled with the
right intentions.

00:40:17.810 --> 00:40:19.790
It's filled with
good intentions.

00:40:19.790 --> 00:40:22.123
But this is why I wanted to
stop you at the first point,

00:40:22.123 --> 00:40:24.165
but now you've given me
two or three other points

00:40:24.165 --> 00:40:24.800
to work with.

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:27.900
So let's walk
through this and see.

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:33.470
First of all, you're
concerned with people

00:40:33.470 --> 00:40:37.880
who have marginal income,
who have difficulty

00:40:37.880 --> 00:40:40.250
with the cost of living.

00:40:40.250 --> 00:40:43.190
You are concerned with their
wages, which would help them

00:40:43.190 --> 00:40:44.750
with their cost of living.

00:40:44.750 --> 00:40:49.200
And you're concerned with the
business owner who hires people

00:40:49.200 --> 00:40:51.620
and what he pays them.

00:40:51.620 --> 00:40:55.135
Those things are interrelated,
intimately interrelated.

00:40:55.135 --> 00:40:56.760
And you see that
because you say, well,

00:40:56.760 --> 00:40:59.300
if we could raise
their income, they

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:04.100
could have more things
that they need in life.

00:41:04.100 --> 00:41:06.710
But the way in which
a business owner

00:41:06.710 --> 00:41:11.090
decides what he
charges someone is

00:41:11.090 --> 00:41:15.440
the same competitive
principle as the wage

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:20.650
earner in terms of what he is
going to buy in a given store.

00:41:20.650 --> 00:41:22.450
In other words, the
price of the good

00:41:22.450 --> 00:41:26.890
that that wage earner
produces, the worker produces

00:41:26.890 --> 00:41:29.350
is not arbitrarily determined.

00:41:29.350 --> 00:41:31.300
It's determined in
context with what

00:41:31.300 --> 00:41:35.050
the supply and the demand
of the service, or the good,

00:41:35.050 --> 00:41:39.100
or the products to
produce the good is.

00:41:39.100 --> 00:41:45.460
In other words, if an
employer arbitrarily

00:41:45.460 --> 00:41:51.920
raised the wage
scale of the worker,

00:41:51.920 --> 00:41:55.870
the worker would produce
goods and services

00:41:55.870 --> 00:42:01.180
that would also have to increase
in order to make ends meet.

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:03.700
That would mean that
all the other workers

00:42:03.700 --> 00:42:06.460
would have to go to the
market and buy those goods

00:42:06.460 --> 00:42:08.390
and services.

00:42:08.390 --> 00:42:12.200
Let me finish, and
then you can come back.

00:42:12.200 --> 00:42:13.780
They would have
to buy those goods

00:42:13.780 --> 00:42:16.600
and services at higher prices.

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:19.720
And they would have to
then go to their employers

00:42:19.720 --> 00:42:23.260
and ask for more higher wages.

00:42:23.260 --> 00:42:27.850
Now if, on the other hand, we
allowed the prices of goods

00:42:27.850 --> 00:42:32.050
and services to exist at
the real level-- that is,

00:42:32.050 --> 00:42:34.010
the real cost of
their production,

00:42:34.010 --> 00:42:38.530
whether it's in terms of
payment for employees,

00:42:38.530 --> 00:42:42.610
or the harvesting of the
resources from society,

00:42:42.610 --> 00:42:45.070
or shipment, or advertising,
whatever else it is--

00:42:45.070 --> 00:42:48.190
if we allow the price
structure to be free,

00:42:48.190 --> 00:42:51.190
then we know what the
real cost of things is.

00:42:51.190 --> 00:42:56.370
If we inflate it arbitrarily,
we distort all that.

00:42:56.370 --> 00:42:58.420
Now, having said all
of that, we still

00:42:58.420 --> 00:43:01.810
have people who have a
hard time making ends meet.

00:43:01.810 --> 00:43:03.825
How are we going to
solve that problem?

00:43:03.825 --> 00:43:05.200
We're going to
solve that problem

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:10.420
by creating as productive and
as wealthy a society as we can,

00:43:10.420 --> 00:43:17.410
where people who are wealthy
can both hire people, but also

00:43:17.410 --> 00:43:20.960
give to those people with
the profits that they have.

00:43:20.960 --> 00:43:22.660
So it's all interrelated.

00:43:22.660 --> 00:43:25.900
And if we pull or obfuscate
any one part of it,

00:43:25.900 --> 00:43:31.330
we throw the whole thing
out of coordination.

00:43:31.330 --> 00:43:35.710
And then what ends up
happening is Venezuela,

00:43:35.710 --> 00:43:37.570
is the Soviet Union,
where you have

00:43:37.570 --> 00:43:40.420
an oversupply of things
that nobody wants

00:43:40.420 --> 00:43:43.430
and an undersupply of things
that people desperately want.

00:43:43.430 --> 00:43:44.414
Go ahead.

00:43:44.414 --> 00:43:46.390
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:49.850 --> 00:43:51.710
SPEAKER 4: Thank you
for the response.

00:43:51.710 --> 00:43:54.850
But I sort of
disagree with the--

00:43:54.850 --> 00:43:57.200
I said, thank you
for the response.

00:43:57.200 --> 00:43:59.490
But I sort of disagree
with the premise,

00:43:59.490 --> 00:44:02.440
so maybe a concrete
example would help.

00:44:02.440 --> 00:44:05.200
So imagine I'm a
business owner--

00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:06.790
I'll use round numbers--

00:44:06.790 --> 00:44:12.430
with 50 employees, who
make $20,000 per year each,

00:44:12.430 --> 00:44:15.160
which is a poverty wage.

00:44:15.160 --> 00:44:21.100
And that business profits
me, say, $500,000 per year.

00:44:21.100 --> 00:44:23.230
I could make the
decision to forego

00:44:23.230 --> 00:44:27.700
a certain amount of that
profit, say, $250,000,

00:44:27.700 --> 00:44:31.700
and pay each of my workers
$5,000 more per year,

00:44:31.700 --> 00:44:34.040
which would alleviate a
lot of their suffering.

00:44:34.040 --> 00:44:38.710
Do you think-- how do
you see that problem?

00:44:38.710 --> 00:44:40.870
What would you advocate
for a business owner to do?

00:44:40.870 --> 00:44:44.735
And that wouldn't raise
the cost of the commodity.

00:44:44.735 --> 00:44:45.610
ROBERT A. SIRICO: OK.

00:44:45.610 --> 00:44:47.660
Mr. Business Owner,
let me ask you this.

00:44:47.660 --> 00:44:51.780
Do you have any ambitions
to grow your business?

00:44:51.780 --> 00:44:53.970
And what portion of
that-- how much did you

00:44:53.970 --> 00:44:56.070
say you were making, $500,000?

00:44:56.070 --> 00:44:56.850
SPEAKER 4: Yeah.

00:44:56.850 --> 00:44:58.560
ROBERT A. SIRICO:
What portion of that

00:44:58.560 --> 00:45:01.080
are you spending on your
boats, and your cars,

00:45:01.080 --> 00:45:05.520
and your rings versus
reinvesting in your business?

00:45:05.520 --> 00:45:08.280
Or let me ask you
a simpler question.

00:45:08.280 --> 00:45:14.680
What is the average
profit of any given

00:45:14.680 --> 00:45:16.460
business in this country?

00:45:16.460 --> 00:45:18.460
You don't have to be
exact, but give me an idea.

00:45:18.460 --> 00:45:20.050
Give me a general idea because--

00:45:22.630 --> 00:45:24.590
what's your
percentage of profit?

00:45:24.590 --> 00:45:28.840
In that $500,000, what's the
percentage of your profit?

00:45:28.840 --> 00:45:30.310
SPEAKER 4: I have no clue.

00:45:30.310 --> 00:45:31.390
Do you have any idea?

00:45:31.390 --> 00:45:33.460
ROBERT A. SIRICO:
Yeah, about 2%.

00:45:33.460 --> 00:45:37.320
I'd invest in a company for 2%.

00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:38.140
Yeah.

00:45:38.140 --> 00:45:43.220
It's 2%, 3%.

00:45:43.220 --> 00:45:45.590
We've got some economists
here who could spit this off

00:45:45.590 --> 00:45:46.770
very easily.

00:45:46.770 --> 00:45:49.460
It's not what you think it is.

00:45:49.460 --> 00:45:50.960
This, by the way--

00:45:50.960 --> 00:45:55.320
I didn't say this here,
but you read my book--

00:45:55.320 --> 00:46:00.650
I was on the left for a number
of years when I was in my 20s.

00:46:00.650 --> 00:46:02.810
I worked with Jane
Fonda and Tom Hayden

00:46:02.810 --> 00:46:04.565
in the Campaign for
Economic Democracy.

00:46:04.565 --> 00:46:05.150
[BOOS]

00:46:05.150 --> 00:46:06.500
Come on, it's my past.

00:46:06.500 --> 00:46:07.765
I've repented.

00:46:07.765 --> 00:46:09.130
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:10.950 --> 00:46:11.700
I've repented.

00:46:11.700 --> 00:46:12.858
I've gone to confession.

00:46:12.858 --> 00:46:14.400
They still want me
to do the penance.

00:46:14.400 --> 00:46:15.312
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:18.050 --> 00:46:21.290
And when I was
talking with somebody,

00:46:21.290 --> 00:46:23.840
the thing he said to
me that shocked me

00:46:23.840 --> 00:46:30.560
was, what do the richest people
do with all of their money?

00:46:30.560 --> 00:46:33.310
I said, well, they have
boats, and they have rings.

00:46:33.310 --> 00:46:34.510
They have cars.

00:46:34.510 --> 00:46:37.450
He said, no, the vast
majority of that money

00:46:37.450 --> 00:46:40.660
is invested in business.

00:46:40.660 --> 00:46:43.210
So what I'm suggesting
to you, if you really

00:46:43.210 --> 00:46:48.500
were that businessperson,
taking half of your profits

00:46:48.500 --> 00:46:52.440
would mean you'd cut your
growth of your business

00:46:52.440 --> 00:46:56.970
by half, which means your goods
and services that you provide

00:46:56.970 --> 00:46:59.040
to the market in
general would go up

00:46:59.040 --> 00:47:02.267
and your employees would lessen.

00:47:02.267 --> 00:47:04.350
In other words, you wouldn't
be able to hire more.

00:47:04.350 --> 00:47:06.660
You wouldn't be able
to hire their cousins

00:47:06.660 --> 00:47:08.950
and their relatives.

00:47:08.950 --> 00:47:11.800
Is that-- OK.

00:47:11.800 --> 00:47:14.850
I'd like to continue, but
we've got other people.

00:47:14.850 --> 00:47:17.980
We can talk afterwards.

00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:19.750
SPEAKER 5: Thank you.

00:47:19.750 --> 00:47:21.760
What you were saying
earlier was basically

00:47:21.760 --> 00:47:24.790
to put on the mind
of Christ, which

00:47:24.790 --> 00:47:28.048
is really hard to do every day.

00:47:28.048 --> 00:47:28.840
Could you give us--

00:47:28.840 --> 00:47:29.550
ROBERT A. SIRICO:
Tell me about it.

00:47:29.550 --> 00:47:30.550
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:31.550 --> 00:47:34.630
I go to confession
every other week.

00:47:34.630 --> 00:47:35.600
SPEAKER 5: Yeah.

00:47:35.600 --> 00:47:38.360
Anyway, I wonder if you
could give us some examples,

00:47:38.360 --> 00:47:42.100
although you did say that doing
the work at the localest level

00:47:42.100 --> 00:47:45.040
is the best way to meet
the needs of the people

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:48.230
and get to know the people
and what their needs are.

00:47:48.230 --> 00:47:50.200
But could you give
us some examples

00:47:50.200 --> 00:47:56.410
of some organizations that
do it right, that do it best?

00:47:56.410 --> 00:47:58.628
What are some good
models for us?

00:47:58.628 --> 00:47:59.670
ROBERT A. SIRICO: I will.

00:47:59.670 --> 00:48:04.510
But let me just clear
up one assumption

00:48:04.510 --> 00:48:07.360
that people always have.

00:48:07.360 --> 00:48:09.547
And here's my real
heresy coming out now.

00:48:09.547 --> 00:48:10.630
Here, this is the big one.

00:48:10.630 --> 00:48:12.700
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:12.700 --> 00:48:18.150
Poverty is not
solved by charity.

00:48:18.150 --> 00:48:21.570
Charity needs to
exist in extreme cases

00:48:21.570 --> 00:48:25.200
when there is a dire
need and whatever.

00:48:25.200 --> 00:48:30.030
Poverty is solved not
by charity, not by even

00:48:30.030 --> 00:48:34.680
the best charities,
but by business,

00:48:34.680 --> 00:48:38.460
the expansion of business,
the globalization of business.

00:48:38.460 --> 00:48:43.500
The world has risen in the last
150, 200 years out of poverty

00:48:43.500 --> 00:48:45.550
at an astounding rate.

00:48:45.550 --> 00:48:46.380
You've seen this.

00:48:46.380 --> 00:48:47.160
This is Hillsdale.

00:48:47.160 --> 00:48:49.290
You've seen the
hockey stick, right?

00:48:49.290 --> 00:48:52.530
You've seen how most people
throughout all of history

00:48:52.530 --> 00:48:53.690
lived at that level.

00:48:53.690 --> 00:48:56.850
And then about 150 years
ago, it began to go up,

00:48:56.850 --> 00:48:59.970
and up, and up, and shoot
right through the roof.

00:48:59.970 --> 00:49:04.350
And what accounts for
that is not charity.

00:49:04.350 --> 00:49:05.700
It's business.

00:49:05.700 --> 00:49:08.340
Business provides jobs.

00:49:08.340 --> 00:49:16.360
The great rabbi-- was a
medieval or Renaissance rabbi--

00:49:16.360 --> 00:49:21.830
Maimonides had this ladder of
charity, how to help people.

00:49:21.830 --> 00:49:26.580
And the top two
levels are give--

00:49:26.580 --> 00:49:30.810
the most moral thing
is give anonymously.

00:49:30.810 --> 00:49:33.795
And then the top level
is render a person--

00:49:37.200 --> 00:49:39.225
render a person without
need of your charity.

00:49:42.450 --> 00:49:46.560
Business well done is
the most charitable thing

00:49:46.560 --> 00:49:47.730
that we can do.

00:49:47.730 --> 00:49:49.973
Now, having said
that, there are tons

00:49:49.973 --> 00:49:52.015
of charitable organizations
in the United States.

00:49:52.015 --> 00:49:54.710
I think Salvation Army
is a good example.

00:49:54.710 --> 00:50:01.350
In my parish, we take up a
second collection every week,

00:50:01.350 --> 00:50:04.530
and that money goes to
the poor, by which we

00:50:04.530 --> 00:50:08.100
mean we have a group of about
20 people in our parish.

00:50:08.100 --> 00:50:12.960
And it's bound by our geography,
so we don't send this money

00:50:12.960 --> 00:50:14.260
all over the world.

00:50:14.260 --> 00:50:16.560
It's just people
we can get to know,

00:50:16.560 --> 00:50:19.110
and it's very leveraged giving.

00:50:19.110 --> 00:50:22.950
So if it's a working family
where the car is broken down,

00:50:22.950 --> 00:50:24.810
we repair the car.

00:50:24.810 --> 00:50:26.610
If it's a family
about to get evicted

00:50:26.610 --> 00:50:29.460
for one reason or another,
we put the deposit down

00:50:29.460 --> 00:50:30.330
on the thing.

00:50:30.330 --> 00:50:33.600
And these people get
to know those people.

00:50:33.600 --> 00:50:35.920
And we invite them
to come to church.

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:37.710
They don't have
to come to church.

00:50:37.710 --> 00:50:39.990
And I've seen a few
people show up in church.

00:50:39.990 --> 00:50:43.160
Sometimes we'll help them with
adoption questions or child

00:50:43.160 --> 00:50:45.490
care questions and
things like that.

00:50:45.490 --> 00:50:49.050
So it's not brain surgery.

00:50:49.050 --> 00:50:51.310
It's just really
getting to know people.

00:50:51.310 --> 00:50:53.270
But there are great
organizations.

00:50:53.270 --> 00:50:54.520
I can give you a list of them.

00:50:57.740 --> 00:50:59.860
SPEAKER 6: So you were
talking earlier about

00:50:59.860 --> 00:51:03.970
how both a free market
framework of business and also

00:51:03.970 --> 00:51:07.540
a local attempt to
give charity is always

00:51:07.540 --> 00:51:10.060
going to be superior to
a top-down government

00:51:10.060 --> 00:51:10.797
institution.

00:51:10.797 --> 00:51:12.380
ROBERT A. SIRICO:
Almost always, yeah.

00:51:12.380 --> 00:51:14.860
I mean, I'd be interested to
hear an example where it isn't.

00:51:14.860 --> 00:51:16.693
SPEAKER 6: No, I
completely agree with that.

00:51:16.693 --> 00:51:17.810
That seems entirely true.

00:51:17.810 --> 00:51:20.830
But in the world
around us right now,

00:51:20.830 --> 00:51:23.950
there is definitely an
increasing encroachment

00:51:23.950 --> 00:51:27.195
of the massive, top-down
institutional government

00:51:27.195 --> 00:51:28.820
structures that you
were talking about.

00:51:28.820 --> 00:51:30.460
And even more than
that, it seems

00:51:30.460 --> 00:51:33.610
like they have a very
different, almost antithetical

00:51:33.610 --> 00:51:35.710
anthropology of what
the human person is

00:51:35.710 --> 00:51:37.090
that's completely different.

00:51:37.090 --> 00:51:38.590
They have completely
different ideas

00:51:38.590 --> 00:51:40.270
of what the human good is.

00:51:40.270 --> 00:51:42.995
And despite a lot of
outspoken voices against it,

00:51:42.995 --> 00:51:44.620
especially recently,
it seems like it's

00:51:44.620 --> 00:51:47.240
growing at a very rapid pace.

00:51:47.240 --> 00:51:50.620
So on the ground, what
are some practical tools

00:51:50.620 --> 00:51:53.200
that you suggest for
fighting this thing?

00:51:53.200 --> 00:51:55.533
It doesn't seem like it's
slowing down at all right now.

00:51:55.533 --> 00:51:56.492
ROBERT A. SIRICO: Yeah.

00:51:56.492 --> 00:51:58.270
Well, I would make
two observations.

00:51:58.270 --> 00:52:00.710
The crisis is horrendous.

00:52:00.710 --> 00:52:03.102
People don't understand
who they are.

00:52:03.102 --> 00:52:05.060
They just really don't
understand who they are.

00:52:05.060 --> 00:52:13.190
If you don't believe me, I
give you drag queen story time.

00:52:13.190 --> 00:52:14.667
I mean, this is just people--

00:52:14.667 --> 00:52:16.250
and I don't mean
just the drag queens.

00:52:16.250 --> 00:52:17.450
Leave them alone.

00:52:17.450 --> 00:52:21.260
I mean the parents who bring
the kids to these things.

00:52:21.260 --> 00:52:28.340
There is a deep anthropological
insanity in our society.

00:52:28.340 --> 00:52:30.140
Now that's the bad side.

00:52:30.140 --> 00:52:33.270
The good side is it can't last.

00:52:33.270 --> 00:52:35.270
But there's another bad side.

00:52:35.270 --> 00:52:38.780
It can last long enough
to do a lot of damage.

00:52:38.780 --> 00:52:41.400
What is the solution to that?

00:52:41.400 --> 00:52:45.870
Hillsdale College, Sacred
Heart Academy, the academies,

00:52:45.870 --> 00:52:48.060
the schools, the
homeschooling parents,

00:52:48.060 --> 00:52:51.510
the other kinds of
colleges like Hillsdale,

00:52:51.510 --> 00:52:54.990
all of these little
platoons, these little groups

00:52:54.990 --> 00:53:01.140
of people who still assure
one another we're not nuts.

00:53:01.140 --> 00:53:02.050
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:02.550 --> 00:53:03.640
They're nuts.

00:53:03.640 --> 00:53:05.104
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:11.450 --> 00:53:14.210
And we have to build
the wreck again.

00:53:14.210 --> 00:53:16.220
We have to build up from it.

00:53:16.220 --> 00:53:18.350
But we are advantaged
in the sense

00:53:18.350 --> 00:53:20.870
that at least we
know some things.

00:53:20.870 --> 00:53:24.095
I mean, at least Adam
Smith wrote his book,

00:53:24.095 --> 00:53:25.160
The Wealth of Nations.

00:53:25.160 --> 00:53:27.240
We know how this thing works.

00:53:27.240 --> 00:53:31.640
So hopefully the collapse
won't be as deep,

00:53:31.640 --> 00:53:33.680
although it frightens me.

00:53:33.680 --> 00:53:35.890
Here's what frightens me.

00:53:35.890 --> 00:53:40.555
And if I offend anybody
here, I can live with that.

00:53:40.555 --> 00:53:41.525
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:42.980 --> 00:53:45.200
No, but seriously,
what's frightening me

00:53:45.200 --> 00:53:47.630
is that some
conservatives are becoming

00:53:47.630 --> 00:53:51.590
very enamored with statist
solutions to things.

00:53:51.590 --> 00:53:55.220
Power tends to corrupt,
and absolute power

00:53:55.220 --> 00:53:56.340
corrupts absolutely.

00:53:56.340 --> 00:53:59.000
And I don't care whether
that's right wing or left wing.

00:53:59.000 --> 00:54:01.130
It's the power you
have to worry about,

00:54:01.130 --> 00:54:04.790
when people get power and
don't let go of power,

00:54:04.790 --> 00:54:08.810
when there isn't a
dispersion of power,

00:54:08.810 --> 00:54:11.420
when there isn't a
kind of subsidiarity.

00:54:11.420 --> 00:54:16.670
And, remember, businesspeople--
and to my colleague, my friend

00:54:16.670 --> 00:54:22.550
there who was talking, I am
not canonizing businesspeople

00:54:22.550 --> 00:54:27.500
as such any more than I'm going
to canonize the poor as such.

00:54:27.500 --> 00:54:29.840
There are businesspeople
who are greedy

00:54:29.840 --> 00:54:34.910
and who make deals with
people who can make deals.

00:54:34.910 --> 00:54:36.000
Nobody comes to me.

00:54:36.000 --> 00:54:37.310
I never get a lobbyist.

00:54:37.310 --> 00:54:38.074
[LAUGHTER]

00:54:38.743 --> 00:54:39.660
They never come to me.

00:54:39.660 --> 00:54:43.070
Now ask yourself why I
don't hear from lobbyists.

00:54:43.070 --> 00:54:45.600
Because I don't have
political power.

00:54:45.600 --> 00:54:46.310
That's why.

00:54:46.310 --> 00:54:50.270
Crony capitalism is
antithetical to real capitalism

00:54:50.270 --> 00:54:51.920
or at least to
real free markets.

00:54:51.920 --> 00:54:53.420
Let's use that word,
because I don't

00:54:53.420 --> 00:54:54.560
like the word "capitalism."

00:54:54.560 --> 00:54:56.480
So these are the things.

00:54:56.480 --> 00:55:02.500
All of this can be
solved by right ideas.

00:55:02.500 --> 00:55:05.260
And so institutions
like this are the place

00:55:05.260 --> 00:55:10.583
to promote those right ideas
and the family, of course.

00:55:10.583 --> 00:55:12.032
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:19.028 --> 00:55:20.820
SPEAKER 7: Thank you
so much for your talk.

00:55:20.820 --> 00:55:23.010
It was really beautiful.

00:55:23.010 --> 00:55:25.290
This question might
be a little bit silly,

00:55:25.290 --> 00:55:27.090
given some of your last answers.

00:55:27.090 --> 00:55:35.580
But given your focus on local
institutions and things,

00:55:35.580 --> 00:55:38.730
would you see distributism as
a viable answer to the welfare

00:55:38.730 --> 00:55:40.650
state?

00:55:40.650 --> 00:55:42.000
ROBERT A. SIRICO: No, I don't.

00:55:42.000 --> 00:55:44.310
I think distributism--
for those of you

00:55:44.310 --> 00:55:47.460
who don't know what
it is, it sees itself

00:55:47.460 --> 00:55:51.690
as a third way between
capitalism and socialism.

00:55:51.690 --> 00:55:55.170
And the articulations, the
best articulations, I suppose,

00:55:55.170 --> 00:55:59.070
of distributism come
from G.K. Chesterton,

00:55:59.070 --> 00:56:03.383
who wrote remarkably little
on the subject, and Belloc.

00:56:03.383 --> 00:56:04.800
And there are a
few other writers,

00:56:04.800 --> 00:56:06.630
but these are the chief
writers that people

00:56:06.630 --> 00:56:08.280
have reference to today.

00:56:08.280 --> 00:56:11.550
I think distributism,
to the extent that it

00:56:11.550 --> 00:56:18.940
is most cogent and persuasive,
is not an economic theory.

00:56:18.940 --> 00:56:20.850
It's an aesthetic theory.

00:56:20.850 --> 00:56:23.220
It tells us that we
can't be mechanized.

00:56:23.220 --> 00:56:28.290
We can't be subordinated
to just systems, that we

00:56:28.290 --> 00:56:31.680
have to be personal,
and creative,

00:56:31.680 --> 00:56:34.980
and cultivate authenticity.

00:56:34.980 --> 00:56:37.330
But that's not an
economic system.

00:56:37.330 --> 00:56:41.460
I think if we put in place
tomorrow a real distributist

00:56:41.460 --> 00:56:44.970
model, even an ideal model
to the extent that Chesterton

00:56:44.970 --> 00:56:46.890
has any practical models--

00:56:46.890 --> 00:56:49.470
which I don't think
he really does--

00:56:49.470 --> 00:56:53.550
I think we would have mass
starvation in the world.

00:56:53.550 --> 00:56:56.070
Because we need
international corporations.

00:56:56.070 --> 00:57:02.190
We need large,
global industries--

00:57:02.190 --> 00:57:07.650
not industries that are bribing
governments or being subsidized

00:57:07.650 --> 00:57:11.790
by governments, but
we need coordination

00:57:11.790 --> 00:57:16.470
internationally through a market
pricing, a free pricing system.

00:57:16.470 --> 00:57:18.680
And distributism
wouldn't allow for that.

00:57:18.680 --> 00:57:20.190
It would make everything small.

00:57:20.190 --> 00:57:24.060
Small is beautiful, according
to the distributists.

00:57:24.060 --> 00:57:27.240
And we have too big a world.

00:57:27.240 --> 00:57:29.980
With more than six billion
people on the planet,

00:57:29.980 --> 00:57:31.800
we need big solutions
to problems.

00:57:31.800 --> 00:57:33.690
We don't need microloans.

00:57:33.690 --> 00:57:35.280
We need macro loans.

00:57:35.280 --> 00:57:38.080
We need big industries.

00:57:38.080 --> 00:57:42.630
And I think that can be done
in a moral way that can also

00:57:42.630 --> 00:57:46.140
have an aesthetic
that can critique

00:57:46.140 --> 00:57:55.020
the kind of soulless
business that the aesthetics

00:57:55.020 --> 00:57:59.790
of distributism
seek to criticize.

00:57:59.790 --> 00:58:03.085
SPEAKER 1: We have time
for one more question.

00:58:03.085 --> 00:58:03.960
SPEAKER 8: Thank you.

00:58:03.960 --> 00:58:05.752
ROBERT A. SIRICO: We're
having so much fun.

00:58:05.752 --> 00:58:07.290
[LAUGHTER]

00:58:07.290 --> 00:58:08.790
SPEAKER 8: It seems
to me that there

00:58:08.790 --> 00:58:13.080
is one word at the
core of any religion

00:58:13.080 --> 00:58:17.160
or at the core of
nature, describes it,

00:58:17.160 --> 00:58:19.560
and that is transformation.

00:58:19.560 --> 00:58:24.620
We are asked to
transform ourselves,

00:58:24.620 --> 00:58:28.350
but not to transform others.

00:58:28.350 --> 00:58:32.250
We can help others to
transform themselves.

00:58:32.250 --> 00:58:34.650
That is what you seem
to be laying out.

00:58:34.650 --> 00:58:36.268
Am I close?

00:58:36.268 --> 00:58:37.560
ROBERT A. SIRICO: You're close.

00:58:37.560 --> 00:58:38.760
You know who put it very well?

00:58:38.760 --> 00:58:40.050
Let me see if I can
get this quote right,

00:58:40.050 --> 00:58:41.508
because this is
one of these quotes

00:58:41.508 --> 00:58:44.730
that I always manage to garble.

00:58:44.730 --> 00:58:53.540
Winston Churchill said that,
"The socialism of Christianity

00:58:53.540 --> 00:58:56.130
said--" no.

00:58:56.130 --> 00:59:03.060
"The socialism of today says,
whatever is yours is mine.

00:59:03.060 --> 00:59:05.760
And the socialism of
Christianity said,

00:59:05.760 --> 00:59:08.680
whatever is mine is yours."

00:59:08.680 --> 00:59:10.110
It's voluntary.

00:59:10.110 --> 00:59:12.358
That's the secret
of the socialism--

00:59:12.358 --> 00:59:14.400
if you want to use that
word, which is anomalous,

00:59:14.400 --> 00:59:16.950
because it's not socialist.

00:59:16.950 --> 00:59:20.410
It's this sense of
sharing and cooperation.

00:59:20.410 --> 00:59:23.530
So I think that's what you were
saying, this transformation.

00:59:23.530 --> 00:59:25.680
Thank you very much for
your time, a pleasure

00:59:25.680 --> 00:59:26.670
being with you.

00:59:26.670 --> 00:59:28.820
[APPLAUSE]

